WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.669
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.669 --> 00:00:11.540
Today we will see how this AI-based technique
can help our virtual characters not only learn

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:16.299
new movements, but they can even perform them
with style.

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:22.460
Now, here you see a piece of reference motion
- this is what we would like our virtual character

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:23.650
to learn.

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:30.009
The task is to then, enter a physics simulation
where try to find the correct joint angles

00:00:30.009 --> 00:00:32.640
and movements to perform that.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:38.149
Of course, this is already a challenge because
even a small difference in joint positions

00:00:38.149 --> 00:00:41.180
can make a great deal of difference in the
output.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:47.160
Then, the second, more difficult task is to
do this with style.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:52.969
No two people perform cartwheels exactly the
same way, so would it be possible to have

00:00:52.969 --> 00:00:58.969
our virtual characters imbued with style,
so that they, much like people, would have

00:00:58.969 --> 00:01:01.370
their own kinds of movement?

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:02.620
Is that possible somehow?

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:07.000
Well, let’s have a look at the simulated
characters.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:12.910
Nice, so this chap surely learned to at the
very least reproduce the reference motion,

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:17.820
but let’s stop the footage here and there
and look for differences.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:21.000
Oh yes, this is indeed different.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:27.600
This virtual character indeed has its own
style, but at the same time, it is still faithful

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.610
to the original reference motion.

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:34.680
This is a magnificent solution to a very difficult
task.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:40.720
And the authors made it look deceptively easy,
but you will see in a moment that this is

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:42.260
really challenging.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:45.990
So how does all this magic happen?

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:49.510
How do we imbue these virtual characters with
style?

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:57.320
Well, let’s define style as a creative deviation
from the reference motion, so it can be different,

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:01.280
but not too different, or else, this happens.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.530
So, what are we seeing here?

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:09.860
Here, with green, you see the algorithm’s
estimation of the center of mass for this

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:10.860
character.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:15.370
And our goal would be to reproduce that as
faithfully as possible.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:17.520
That would be the copying machine solution.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.790
But, here comes the key for style.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:23.970
And that key is using spacetime bounds.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:29.390
This means that the center of mass of the
character can deviate from the original, but

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:34.390
only as long as it remains strictly within
these boundaries.

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:39.300
And that is where the style emerges!

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:45.080
If we wish to add a little style to the equation,
we can set relatively loose spacetime bounds

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:49.610
around it, leaving room for the AI to explore.

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:55.240
If we wish to strictly reproduce the reference
motion, we can set the bounds to be really

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.060
tight, instead.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:03.710
This is a great technique to learn running,
jumping, rolling behaviors, it can even perform

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:08.000
a stylish cartwheel and backflips.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.220
Oh yeah.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:11.220
Loving it.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:17.180
These spacetime bounds also help us retarget
the motion to different virtual body types.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:22.560
And furthermore, it also helps us salvage
really bad quality reference motions and make

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.490
something useful out of them.

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:27.770
So, are we done here?

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:28.940
Is that all?

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:31.340
No, not in the slightest!

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:35.980
Now, hold on to your papers because here comes
the best part.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:41.090
With these novel spacetime bounds, we can
specify additional stylistic choices to the

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:42.540
character moves.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:48.270
For instance, we can encourage the character
to use more energy for a more intense dancing

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:57.680
sequence, or, we can make it sleepier by asking
it to decrease its energy use.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:04.040
And I wonder if we can put bounds on the energy
use, can we do more, for instance, do the

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.480
same with, for instance, body volume use.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:07.870
Oh yeah!

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:13.160
This really opens up new kinds of motions
that I haven’t seen virtual characters perform

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:14.160
yet.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:20.010
For instance, this chap was encouraged to
use its entire body volume for a walk, and

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:24.540
thus, looks like someone who is clearly looking
for trouble.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:33.780
And this poor thing just finished their paper
for a conference deadline and is barely alive.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:37.130
We can even mix multiple motions together.

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:42.780
For instance, what could be a combination
of a regular running sequence, and a bent

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:44.710
walk?

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:48.550
Well, this.

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:54.810
And if we have a standard running sequence,
and a happy walk, we can fuse them into a

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:56.980
happy running sequence.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:58.520
How cool is that?

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:04.770
So, with this technique, we can finally not
only teach virtual characters to perform nearly

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:11.520
any kind of reference motion, but we can even
ask them to do these with style.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:13.630
What an incredible idea.

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:14.660
Loving it!

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:16.500
Now, before we go.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:21.000
I would like to show you a short message that
we got that melted my heart.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:26.780
This I got from Nathan, who has been inspired
by these incredible works and he decided to

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:31.020
turn his life around, and go back to study
more.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:37.410
I love my job, and reading messages like this
is one of the absolute best parts of it.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:41.419
Congratulations Nathan, thank you so much,
and good luck!

00:05:41.419 --> 00:05:46.400
If you feel that you have a similar story
with this video series, make sure to let us

00:05:46.400 --> 00:06:16.470
know in 

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:38.340
the comments!

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:41.800
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

